    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_3317d04e5097fa4d673c04fbcb75191e107ef9c98abfd54ac595fbb28b4ba7f4",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source fact: for two primitive-character L-functions, the passage separately asks about coincident zeros with the same multiplicity, bracketing of a zero from one ordered ordinate sequence by consecutive ordinates of the other, and the integer A_n=n-m determined by that bracketing. Interpretation: ordering turns comparison of zero sets into sequence geometry, and A_n compresses relative index placement without identifying coincidence, interlacing, and displacement. Boundary: this unit poses questions and definitions; it proves no implication among them. Uncertainty: OCR corrupts the L/character/ordinate symbols and subscripts, so exact notation and repeated-ordinate conventions require the scan.",
